C = [
("h1", "15. The Grounds Side by Side"),
("p", "Claim: when Paul’s instructions are set together, their grounds fall into kinds, and the kinds are not interchangeable. An instruction that rests on creation does not rest on custom, and an instruction that rests on the character of God does not rest on a local fault. The table in this chapter is the volume’s evidence for its thesis."),
("sub", "15.1 The kinds of ground"),
("p", "Seven kinds of ground appear in the passages the volume has read. The first is the nature of the Body: one body, many members, God’s setting (1 Corinthians 12). The second is the Giver: the ascended Christ’s gifts for the edifying of the body (Ephesians 4). The third is the character of God: “not the author of confusion, but of peace” (1 Corinthians 14:33). The fourth is Scripture’s account of the beginning: headship and creation in 1 Corinthians 11, “the law” in 14:34, and Adam first formed and Eve deceived in 1 Timothy 2:13, 14. The fifth is Paul’s own authority: “the commandments of the Lord” (1 Corinthians 14:37) and “as I had appointed thee” (Titus 1:5). The sixth is nature, propriety and the practice of the churches (1 Corinthians 11:13–16). The seventh is effect: that the spirit be saved, that the lump not be leavened, that a brother be ashamed, that others fear (1 Corinthians 5; 2 Thessalonians 3:14; 1 Timothy 5:20)."),
("sub", "15.2 Which grounds are local"),
("p", "A ground is local if it is about the place where the instruction was given and would not hold elsewhere. Paul gives some grounds of that kind. “And ye are puffed up” (1 Corinthians 5:2) is a fault of Corinth. The shame and propriety of 1 Corinthians 11:13–15 are in the judgment of the Corinthians (“Judge in yourselves”). The practice of “the churches of God” in 11:16 is a practice of the churches of that time. The volume does not say that these grounds are without force. It says that they are the kind that Paul himself marks as depending on a place and a people."),
("p", "Other grounds are not local on their face. The nature of the Body is not Corinth’s. The ascended Christ’s gifts are not Ephesus’s alone. “God is not the author of confusion, but of peace, as in all churches of the saints” is a statement about God and about all churches. The creation of Adam first and the deception of Eve are not events of Ephesus. “The commandments of the Lord” in 14:37 is a claim about the kind of instruction Paul writes. The volume argues that the instructions resting on these grounds are not to be set aside because the church of Corinth or Ephesus was a particular church. The argument is from Paul’s own statement of the ground, and not from a view of what is convenient or what is customary."),
("ctbl", "Every instruction, its ground, and the kind of ground.", [
    ("Instruction", "The ground Paul states", "The kind of ground"),
    ("Members are set in the body as God pleased", "“God hath tempered the body together” (1 Corinthians 12:24)", "The nature of the Body"),
    ("Gifts for the work of the ministry and the edifying of the body", "“the measure of the gift of Christ” (Ephesians 4:7)", "The Giver"),
    ("Order in the meeting, limits on number and turn", "“God is not the author of confusion” (1 Corinthians 14:33)", "The character of God"),
    ("Appoint elders; qualifications for the bishop", "“as I had appointed thee” (Titus 1:5); “as the steward of God” (1:7)", "Paul’s authority; the nature of the stewardship"),
    ("Women pray and prophesy covered; men uncovered", "The order of heads; creation; angels; nature; the churches’ practice (1 Corinthians 11)", "Creation and order, and also nature and custom"),
    ("Women keep silence in the churches", "“under obedience, as also saith the law” (1 Corinthians 14:34)", "Scripture’s account of the beginning"),
    ("A woman not to teach or usurp authority over the man", "“Adam was first formed, then Eve” (1 Timothy 2:13, 14)", "Scripture’s account of the beginning"),
    ("Put away the man who has his father’s wife", "“a little leaven leaveneth the whole lump”; that the spirit may be saved (1 Corinthians 5)", "Effect"),
    ("Withdraw from the disorderly brother", "“that he may be ashamed” (2 Thessalonians 3:14)", "Effect"),
    ("Rebuke those who sin before all", "“that others also may fear” (1 Timothy 5:20)", "Effect"),
  ], [0.32, 0.42, 0.26]),
("sub", "15.3 What the table shows"),
("p", "The table shows that no single ground carries all of Paul’s instructions. A reader who says that every instruction about the assembly rests on creation will find that the instructions on discipline rest on effect. A reader who says that every instruction rests on custom will find that the instruction on the order of the meeting rests on the character of God. A reader who says that every instruction rests on order in the abstract will find that Paul’s “in order” (1 Corinthians 14:40) sums up what the earlier verses ground in edifying and in peace, and is not a ground of its own."),
("p", "It shows also that the instructions on women stand apart in one respect: the two that rest on Scripture’s account of the beginning, 1 Corinthians 14:34 and 1 Timothy 2:13, 14, are the two in which Paul uses the language of what he does not “permit” or “suffer,” and the one that rests partly on nature and custom, 1 Corinthians 11, is the one in which he uses the language of what is comely. The volume reports the difference. It does not say that the second is trivial. It says that the first kind is not tied by Paul to a place."),
("sub", "15.4 What the volume takes from the chapter"),
("bul", [
    "Paul’s grounds are of different kinds: the Body, the Giver, God’s character, creation and the law, his own authority, nature and custom, and effect.",
    "Some grounds are marked by Paul as local; others are not.",
    "No one ground carries all the instructions, and the table is the evidence.",
    "The instructions on women rest on different grounds in different passages, and the volume reports which.",
  ]),
]
